Publication‑Ready Abstract — Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE)

Author: C. Matthew BlackwellSystem: Astrometric Simulation Engine — Proton‑Laser Interaction & Cosmic Data Integration Framework

Abstract

The Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE) presents a unified, multi‑scale computational architecture that integrates astrophysical environmental datasets with high‑energy proton–laser interaction physics inside a high‑performance, zero‑trust‑secured simulation framework. ASE ingests heterogeneous cosmic data—including HDF5 astrophysics archives, FITS telescope imagery, GEANT4 particle‑interaction datasets, cosmic‑ray spectra, star‑formation models, and interstellar medium (ISM) density maps—and transforms them into standardized boundary conditions for microphysical plasma simulations. This preprocessing pipeline enables laser–plasma interaction studies to be conducted within astrophysically realistic environments, bridging a gap between observational cosmology and high‑energy plasma physics.

The core engine employs a hybrid solver architecture combining a Particle‑in‑Cell (PIC) kinetic model, Vlasov–Maxwell electromagnetic field evolution, charge‑conserving Esirkepov/Yee interpolation, relativistic Boris/Yaw particle pushing, and collision operators for particle–field and particle–particle interactions. A magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) coupling module extends the simulation to large‑scale magnetic structures, allowing proton acceleration mechanisms—including Target Normal Sheath Acceleration (TNSA) and Radiation Pressure Acceleration (RPA)—to be modeled under astrophysical field conditions.

ASE is optimized for distributed high‑performance computing environments through MPI/OpenMP parallelization, CUDA/HIP GPU acceleration, InfiniBand interconnects, and Lustre/GPUDirect storage, enabling ensemble simulations, parameter sweeps, and real‑time cluster orchestration. A zero‑trust security subsystem provides AES‑256 data‑at‑rest encryption, TLS 1.3 data‑in‑transit protection, Merkle‑tree integrity verification, hardware security module (HSM) integration, and role‑based access control (RBAC), ensuring confidentiality, reproducibility, and compliance for sensitive scientific workflows.

A Qt/Python‑based Simulation Designer GUI supports interactive configuration, 3D visualization, data slicing, and comparative analysis across cosmic and plasma domains. Together, these components establish ASE as a holistic, research‑grade simulation platform capable of modeling proton–laser dynamics within astrophysical environments. The system provides a novel computational foundation for investigations in cosmic‑ray acceleration, plasma astrophysics, laser‑driven proton dynamics, and high‑energy particle transport across interstellar structures.

